        "dimension": "high_level_elegance",
        "related_files": [
          "app/eventyay/base/admin.py"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "Line 5-13 of base/admin.py: 'from .models import (Announcement, AuditLog, auth, base, BBBCall, BBBServer, BillingInvoice, billing, Channel, ChatEvent, checkin, choices, Device, devices, event, ...)' \u2014 'auth', 'base', 'billing', 'checkin', 'choices', 'devices', 'event', 'fields', 'giftcards', 'invoices', 'log', 'notifications', 'orders', 'organizer', 'page', 'poll', 'poster', 'room', 'roomquestion', 'roulette', 'seating', 'tax', 'vouchers', 'waitinglist', 'exhibitor', 'chat' are submodules; 'Announcement', 'BBBCall', 'BBBServer' are classes.",
          "This pattern means the admin.py is bypassing the models/__init__.py public surface to access internal module namespaces directly, undermining the purpose of the facade.",
          "The inconsistency means some models are registered as 'admin.site.register(auth.User)' while others are 'admin.site.register(Order)' \u2014 there is no single consistent style."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Standardize admin.py to import exclusively from the models/__init__.py public surface (e.g., 'from .models import User, U2FDevice, Order, ...') rather than mixing module and class imports. If the public surface is missing some registerable models, add them to __init__.py. This localizes the admin.py's dependency on a single stable interface.",
