    "review::.::holistic::low_level_elegance::perform_operations_monolith": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::low_level_elegance::perform_operations_monolith",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "OrderChangeManager._perform_operations is a 293-line if/elif dispatch chain with inlined log+mutate blocks for each operation type",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "low_level_elegance",
        "related_files": [
          "app/eventyay/base/services/orders.py"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "_perform_operations() (lines ~1921-2213) contains one large elif branch per operation type (ProductOperation, SeatOperation, SubeventOperation, AddFeeOperation, FeeValueOperation, PriceOperation, TaxRuleOperation, CancelFeeOperation, CancelOperation, AddOperation, SplitOperation, RegenerateSecretOperation), each inlining order.log_action + field mutations + save",
          "The natural abstraction \u2014 'apply this operation to the order' \u2014 has a name for each operation type (ProductOperation, etc.) but the execution logic lives in an anonymous elif rather than a method on the namedtuple or a dedicated handler"
        ],
        "suggestion": "Extract a handler per operation type: e.g. _apply_product_operation(op), _apply_seat_operation(op), etc. Each handler performs the log_action + mutation + save for that type. _perform_operations() becomes a short dispatch loop over self._operations calling the appropriate handler. This reduces the function from ~293 lines to ~20 lines and makes each operation's logic independently readable and testable.",
        "reasoning": "",
