      "tier": 2,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "Permission strings ('can_view_orders', 'can_change_orders', etc.) are scattered as magic literals across view and API files instead of shared constants",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "authorization_consistency",
        "related_files": [
          "app/eventyay/control/views/checkin.py",
          "app/eventyay/control/views/orders.py",
          "app/eventyay/api/auth/permission.py",
          "app/eventyay/base/models/organizer.py"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "The strings 'can_view_orders', 'can_change_orders', 'can_change_items', 'can_change_event_settings', etc. appear as inline string literals in `permission =` attributes and `has_event_permission()` calls across 20+ view files.",
          "Team model in organizer.py defines these as boolean fields (line 367-378) and has a TEAM_PERMISSIONS dict (line 295), but this dict maps to rules functions, not the permission string constants \u2014 no importable string constants exist.",
          "A typo in any one view's permission string silently bypasses auth enforcement (the mixin would see the unknown permission as falsy) with no static analysis to catch it."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Define permission name constants (e.g., PERM_VIEW_ORDERS = 'can_view_orders') in a shared module such as eventyay/base/permissions.py or eventyay/control/permissions.py, and import them wherever permission strings are used. This eliminates typo risk and enables static analysis.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "summary_hash": "19bcf22a"
      },
      "status": "wontfix",
      "note": "Permission strings are a Django convention; extracting to constants adds indirection without benefit in this codebase.",
      "first_seen": "2026-03-14T01:19:56+00:00",
      "last_seen": "2026-03-14T01:19:56+00:00",
